HTTP vs HTTPS - Quick Comparison
Feature	HTTP	HTTPS
Security	❌ Unencrypted	✅ Encrypted
Port	80	443
Can you see content?	✅ Yes, in plain text	❌ No, it's encrypted
Can you see headers?	✅ Yes	❌ No
What Wireshark shows	Full request/response	Only IPs and ports

HTTPS filter: 
1.	Filter: tls or ssl
2.	You'll see encrypted traffic
3.	You CAN'T see the actual content (because of encryption!)
4.	You CAN see the handshake (certificate exchange)
Note: This is the MOST useful Wireshark feature for HTTP:
1.	Right-click any HTTP packet
2.	Select Follow → TCP Stream
3.	You'll see the ENTIRE conversation:
o	All the requests your PC made
o	All the responses from the server
o	The actual HTML/JSON/Image data
4.	Use the dropdown to view in:
o	ASCII (readable text)
o	Hex Dump (for images/binary)
o	C Arrays (for developers)
 
TCP/the Three-way Handshake Filter
What is TCP?
•	Transmission Control Protocol
•	Reliable, connection-oriented protocol
•	Used by HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, email, etc.
•	Guarantees data arrives correctly

Sequence Number (SEQ):
•	A number that labels each byte of data
•	Starts with a random number (the ISN - Initial Sequence Number)
•	Increases by the amount of data sent
•	Used to reassemble data in correct order
Acknowledgment Number (ACK):
•	"I have received up to this sequence number"
•	Next expected byte from the other side
•	Confirms receipt of data
 
TCP Error Detection - Retransmissions:
•	If a packet is lost, the sender will resend it
•	Wireshark will show "TCP Retransmission" in Info column
•	This is a sign of network problems (packet loss, congestion, etc.)

The TCP 3-Way Handshake (Connection Setup):
Every TCP connection starts with this exact sequence:
Step	Packet	Direction	What It Means
1	[SYN]	Client → Server	"Hello, I want to connect. Here's my initial sequence number."
2	[SYN, ACK]	Server → Client	"Hello back! I agree to connect. Here's MY initial sequence number AND I'm acknowledging yours."
3	[ACK]	Client → Server	"Great! I acknowledge YOUR sequence number. Connection is now established."
